   "created_at": "2018-01-14T20:49:29Z",
   "updated_at": "2018-01-14T20:49:29Z",
   "body": "I agree that this would be useful -- but I think there are two big issues here compared to something like SSL Labs:\r\n\r\n* Maintenance over time. One of the things that makes SSL Labs so powerful is that it raises its standards each year as the world changes, while always keeping an A/A+ an achievable grade. \r\n\r\n* Pshtt only looks at a few things, really -- it's an HTTP/HTTPS/HSTS behavior analyzer. It doesn't look at other aspects of TLS connection security like sslyze or SSL Labs do. SSL Labs, in particular, does a very thorough job of attempting/simulating connections from various client profiles, particularly needed with different levels of cipher/protocol support out there, and different root stores.\r\n\r\nJust my opinion, but I think given pshtt's limited scope, it's unlikely we could give a grade that would be a useful/living \"stopping point\" for people looking to tighten up their HTTPS security.",
   "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
   "pin": null,
